For the Pantheon’s Dark of the Senses demo, I’d make this the launch criterion: people feel the loss of a cue before they can name what disappeared.

Start with a well-lit hallway. Let viewers hear footsteps in sync with the walker. Halfway down, remove just the footsteps. Keep the gait, lighting, and room tone steady. Make a control version where the footsteps continue, and give each viewer one version before asking what they felt.

Gate	Pass if
The cue takes hold	Viewers experience the opening as an ordinary walk.
Its absence changes the scene	More viewers in the cue-removal group report unease or disorientation than in the control group, before you mention sound.
The effect stays specific	After they describe what they felt, viewers can locate roughly when the scene changed. Check both headphones and phone speakers.
The idea travels	A second demo works through another sense. In a tabletop version, let someone repeat a motion by feel, then remove the familiar stopping point while keeping the rest of the setup steady.

Launch the hallway as the first demo once it clears the first three gates. The fourth is the bar for calling it a cross-sensory Pantheon principle.

The first question I’d ask after playback: “When did the hallway start to feel different?” Ask what they heard only after they’ve answered.